# Engage MT — environment example.
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored.

# ArcGIS Online API key (only required when consuming items that ArcGIS Online
# protects with a key; public Hub layers do not require one for Phase 1).
# VITE_ARCGIS_API_KEY=

# MyFWP / XMT OAuth client id (Phase 4). Until then, stubs return STUB-OAUTH-DO-NOT-SEND.
# VITE_MYFWP_CLIENT_ID=
# VITE_MYFWP_OAUTH_AUTHORIZE_URL=
# VITE_MYFWP_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=

# FWP Regs Manager API base — the app reads DEA hunting regulations live from
# here (fetchHuntingRegs). MUST include the
# /api/v1/fwp route prefix. Unset → the app falls back to the offline snapshot
# (mobile/offline-first behaviour). The production web Docker image bakes this in
# via a build ARG (see root Dockerfile); set it here only for local REST testing.
# VITE_FWP_API_BASE=https://fwp-regs-manager.up.railway.app/api/v1/fwp

# ─── BUILD-TIME ONLY (never shipped to the browser) ──────────────────────────
# Recreation.gov (RIDB) API key. Used solely by the data build scripts
# (`npm run data:crosswalk` / `data:campgrounds`) to deep-link federal
# recreation sites + campgrounds to their exact recreation.gov facility pages.
# Free — create a recreation.gov account, enable developer access, and copy the
# key from your profile at https://ridb.recreation.gov/profile. Passed to RIDB
# as an `apikey` header (rate limit 50 req/min). Deliberately NOT VITE_-prefixed
# so it can never reach the client bundle — the crosswalk it produces is a
# bundled, same-origin JSON (privacy rule + offline-safe). Keyless builds still
# succeed (they keep the committed crosswalk / fall back to search URLs).
# RIDB_API_KEY=
